   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,470:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,471:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 1 × 500 Ω
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,472:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,473:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 1 × Switch
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,474:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: DIFFICULTY: ★☆☆☆☆
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,475:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Tutorial 8: Series circuits
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,476:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Did you notice that in all the challenges you
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,476:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,479:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: ve solved so far, your circuits were all just a single loop? We call
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,480:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: circuits like these 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,480:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: "
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,481:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: series circuits
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,482:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: "
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,485:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  because they are a series of parts connected one after the other (when you draw
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,486:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: them in a circuit diagram).
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,494:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,503:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://pplines-online.bj.bcebos.com/deploy/official/paddleocr/pp-ocr-vl-16-online//c6e57968-1cec-4871-8eb7-37264c8234b7/markdown_2/imgs/img_in_image_box_226_472_987_953.jpg?authorization=bce-auth-v1%2FALTAKDN8mY5KlNI7zaRpLmOqrw%2F2026-08-11T07%3A56%3A31Z%2F-1%2F%2F9334c76c195b155b002c978c7129deeccfa0cd834d5af9bf2b2ca5bb7fe5ae70" alt="Image" width="63%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,505:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: In series circuits, you can
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,506:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,510:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: t turn one part without turning all the others. Also, in series circuits, all of the chain moves
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,511:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: the same speed. In other words, 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,513:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: the current is the same everywhere in a series circuit
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,513:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: .
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,514:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Now, can you think of a way to connect these parts together so that they 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,515:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: aren
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,516:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,516:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: t
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,517:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  in series? Neither can I. Not yet,
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,518:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: anyway.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,519:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: NEW PART UNLOCKED
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,527:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,535:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://pplines-online.bj.bcebos.com/deploy/official/paddleocr/pp-ocr-vl-16-online//c6e57968-1cec-4871-8eb7-37264c8234b7/markdown_3/imgs/img_in_image_box_158_306_573_663.jpg?authorization=bce-auth-v1%2FALTAKDN8mY5KlNI7zaRpLmOqrw%2F2026-08-11T07%3A56%3A32Z%2F-1%2F%2Fc41ccb9adead3ac584f2d8aa19385f839c9cbd57e3c00638a239d7d1bfa697fe" alt="Image" width="33%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,538:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <p style="text-align: center;"><b>THE SPINTRONIC JUNCTION</b></p>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,539:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: PURPOSE:
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,540:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: THE JUNCTION ALLOWS CURRENT IN ONE LOOP TO SPLIT INTO TWO LOOPS.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,541:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: DETAIL:
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,542:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: THIS IS JUST LIKE AN ELECTRONIC JUNCTION, WHERE ONE WIRE SPLITS INTO TWO. IT
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,543:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,545:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: S AN
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,546:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: EXTREMELY USEFUL PART, BUT VERY DIFFICULT TO MASTER.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,547:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Tutorial 9: Spintronic junctions
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,549:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Spintronic junctions allow current in one loop to split into two loops. Think of it like a junction between three pipes:
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,551:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,552:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://i.imgur.com/P3CkT4J.png" width="70%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,553:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: It
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,554:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,555:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: s the same with a spintronic junction.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,557:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,558:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://i.imgur.com/7ngYGvU.png" width="70%"/></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,559:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: You can push current into 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,560:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: any
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,568:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  of the three sprockets and it will split between the other two. Junctions are
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,570:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: mind-bending, but they make it possible to build just about any electronic circuit.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,572:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: CHALLENGE 17: FIX THE SHORT CIRCUIT
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,573:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Fix the short.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,574:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: NOTE: WATCH OUT FOR SHORTS WHEN YOU USE JUNCTIONS. It
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,575:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,576:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: s EASY TO ACCIDENTALLY MAKE
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,576:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: THEM.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,577:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: The number of little gray dots on the chains show the sprocket they
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,578:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,578:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: re meant to be on:
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:24,580:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
