   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,055:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: This one
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,056:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,057:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: s 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,058:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: tricky
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,059:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: . Of course I could make it with two junctions, but I can also make it with 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,060:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: just one
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,061:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: !
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,062:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Think of this section of the circuit as 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,063:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: one unit
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,064:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  that
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,064:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,076:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: s in series with the 200 Ω resistor. In the unit, one loop of the
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,080:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: junction goes to the 500 Ω resistor and back. Another loop goes to the 1000 Ω resistor and back.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,080:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: *
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,084:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
