   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,537:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Tutorial 12: Voltage
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,539:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Voltage is a measure of how hard the chain pulls on a part in the circuit. Voltage is measured in 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,539:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: "
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,540:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: spin volts
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,541:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: "
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,542:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: , or 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,542:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: "
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,543:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: V
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,544:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: "
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,552:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: .
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,555:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Spintronic capacitors can be used to measure the voltage. In the picture below, a sprocket pushes on the capacitor
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,556:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: with +6 V.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,559:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,560:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://i.imgur.com/i67pD3d.png" alt="Image" width="47%"
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,561:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,562:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: And here, the sprocket doesn
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,563:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,564:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: t push at all. The spintronic capacitor shows 0 V.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,573:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,581:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://pplines-online.bj.bcebos.com/deploy/official/paddleocr/pp-ocr-vl-16-online//4e2ca0ba-c8a5-4bbd-bb8f-6c9815271f93/markdown_1/imgs/img_in_image_box_240_724_818_896.jpg?authorization=bce-auth-v1%2FALTAKDN8mY5KlNI7zaRpLmOqrw%2F2026-08-11T07%3A56%3A32Z%2F-1%2F%2F95008eb71a67bc776e9104f01d4c6917fcf54320171a008ed3dcc9b426d66432" alt="Image" width="47%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,584:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: When parts are pushed in a counterclockwise direction, they feel a negative voltage. Here, the sprocket pushes with
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,585:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: -6 V.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,593:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,602:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://pplines-online.bj.bcebos.com/deploy/official/paddleocr/pp-ocr-vl-16-online//4e2ca0ba-c8a5-4bbd-bb8f-6c9815271f93/markdown_1/imgs/img_in_image_box_333_1026_818_1194.jpg?authorization=bce-auth-v1%2FALTAKDN8mY5KlNI7zaRpLmOqrw%2F2026-08-11T07%3A56%3A32Z%2F-1%2F%2Fc3085161a35ab3d460dccd96e58ace97ae16a343cb59d1349a58d093e279c94a" alt="Image" width="39%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,614:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: In the next challenges, voltage and current are extremely important. To truly understand a circuit, you need to
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,616:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: understand how voltage and current at each point in a circuit change over time.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,617:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: *
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,618:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: One spin volit is equal to 0.1 newtons.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,620:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: CHALLENGE 42: MEASURE THE BATTERY
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,621:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,622:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: S VOLTAGE
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,625:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: MEASURE THE VOLTAGE PRODUCED BY THE BATTERY. USE A RESISTOR TO KEEP IT FROM
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,626:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: OVERSHOOTING THE TRUE VOLTAGE.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,627:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Hmm...sometimes the capacitor 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,628:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: gets a little stuck
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,629:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  and doesn
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,630:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,631:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: t show an 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,632:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: accurate voltage
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,633:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: .
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,634:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Aha
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,634:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: !
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,636:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  To get the capacitor to show an accurate voltage, give it a little 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,636:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: tap
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,637:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,638:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: tap
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,639:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,639:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: tap
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,640:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  on top with your finger until it
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,641:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: doesn
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,641:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,642:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: t move anymore.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,643:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: STARTING SETUP
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,651:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,660:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://pplines-online.bj.bcebos.com/deploy/official/paddleocr/pp-ocr-vl-16-online//4e2ca0ba-c8a5-4bbd-bb8f-6c9815271f93/markdown_2/imgs/img_in_image_box_208_940_547_1538.jpg?authorization=bce-auth-v1%2FALTAKDN8mY5KlNI7zaRpLmOqrw%2F2026-08-11T07%3A56%3A32Z%2F-1%2F%2Fd64e202ecc4581b26e0ab29f785d2d5b85daec2c23d33ba90fbacfccd6cc4928" alt="Image" width="27%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,662:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: AVAILABLE PARTS
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,663:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,665:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 1 × 200 Ω
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,666:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,667:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 1 × Chain
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,667:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,668:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 1 × Capacitor (0.001 F)
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,670:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: DIFFICULTY: ★☆☆☆☆
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,672:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: CHALLENGE 43: NEGATIVE VOLTAGE
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,673:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: THE BATTERY
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,673:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,679:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: S VOLTAGE SHOULD HAVE BEEN ROUGHLY +6 V. NOW BUILD A CIRCUIT THAT
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,681:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: SETS THE VOLTAGE ACROSS THE CAPACITOR C1 TO ROUGHLY -6 V.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,682:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: STARTING SETUP
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,690:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,699:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://pplines-online.bj.bcebos.com/deploy/official/paddleocr/pp-ocr-vl-16-online//4e2ca0ba-c8a5-4bbd-bb8f-6c9815271f93/markdown_3/imgs/img_in_image_box_236_929_616_1539.jpg?authorization=bce-auth-v1%2FALTAKDN8mY5KlNI7zaRpLmOqrw%2F2026-08-11T07%3A56%3A33Z%2F-1%2F%2Fff6931c3f5d8cb7990fcb2b486af8a774a47e74e08b3772da9e83095bf9fffaf" alt="Image" width="31%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,700:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: AVAILABLE PARTS
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,701:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,702:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 1 × 200 Ω
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,703:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,704:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 1 × Chain
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,705:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: DIFFICULTY: ★★☆☆☆
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,706:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Tutorial 13: Voltage at Junctions
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,706:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: I
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,707:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,709:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: m getting even more comfortable with junctions, especially now that I figured out this little fact: 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,711:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: The voltages on
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,713:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: the three sprockets of a junction are always equal.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,714:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  Always
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,715:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: !
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,716:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Start by building this parallel circuit:
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,726:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,737:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://pplines-online.bj.bcebos.com/deploy/official/paddleocr/pp-ocr-vl-16-online//89752177-4ead-46d8-b2ca-5f97ab47c47c/markdown_0/imgs/img_in_image_box_729_378_1039_765.jpg?authorization=bce-auth-v1%2FALTAKDN8mY5KlNI7zaRpLmOqrw%2F2026-08-11T07%3A56%3A31Z%2F-1%2F%2F9543ec647b0c4116c7ee87e11b3cb92460acde8f239c55c96fa5cbb967cce3b3" alt="Image" width="25%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,739:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: The voltage pushing the bottom sprocket is 6 V (from the battery). 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,741:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: What is the voltage pushing the other two
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,742:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: sprockets?
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,743:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  Replace each resistor with the voltmeter to find out.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,752:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,764:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://pplines-online.bj.bcebos.com/deploy/official/paddleocr/pp-ocr-vl-16-online//89752177-4ead-46d8-b2ca-5f97ab47c47c/markdown_0/imgs/img_in_image_box_277_964_556_1315.jpg?authorization=bce-auth-v1%2FALTAKDN8mY5KlNI7zaRpLmOqrw%2F2026-08-11T07%3A56%3A31Z%2F-1%2F%2Fa29682f68fbc054231884c23eeac7c4fd35d6514f58ed561b0134bf7bedd79de" alt="Image" width="22%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,775:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,784:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://pplines-online.bj.bcebos.com/deploy/official/paddleocr/pp-ocr-vl-16-online//89752177-4ead-46d8-b2ca-5f97ab47c47c/markdown_0/imgs/img_in_image_box_713_966_990_1312.jpg?authorization=bce-auth-v1%2FALTAKDN8mY5KlNI7zaRpLmOqrw%2F2026-08-11T07%3A56%3A31Z%2F-1%2F%2Fb5e775364e41038d9ec024ba103e469e042f8027c5bbff3fa9d888f5721533d1" alt="Image" width="22%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,787:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: What do you measure? 6 V, right? It
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,788:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,792:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: s always the same no matter what. The three sprockets of a junction always
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,793:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: push with the same voltage
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,794:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: !
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,795:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Wait a second, if the voltage is the same, why does the 500 Ω resistor turn 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,796:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: twice as fast
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,797:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  as the 1000 Ω resistor?
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,807:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,816:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://pplines-online.bj.bcebos.com/deploy/official/paddleocr/pp-ocr-vl-16-online//89752177-4ead-46d8-b2ca-5f97ab47c47c/markdown_1/imgs/img_in_image_box_699_195_1003_592.jpg?authorization=bce-auth-v1%2FALTAKDN8mY5KlNI7zaRpLmOqrw%2F2026-08-11T07%3A56%3A32Z%2F-1%2F%2F1e58c890a333835c3677f9e6efcabae0bc7ad91ce92282cbb8122463a042867e" alt="Image" width="24%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,818:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Oh yeah
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,819:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: !
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,820:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  It
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,821:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,822:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: s because 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,823:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: voltage
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,823:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  is just the amount of 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,824:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: force
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,825:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  pushing on the sprockets, not how fast they
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,826:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,831:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: re actually
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,833:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: turning. The voltage on each resistor is the same, but the resistors turn at different speeds.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,835:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: At a junction, the voltage on each of the three sprockets is always the same, but the current may be different.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,837:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: CHALLENGE 44: IS THE BATTERY VOLTAGE CONSTANT?
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,839:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: IN THE CIRCUIT BELOW, THE BATTERY SLOWLY DISCHARGES INTO R1. DOES THE BATTERY
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,840:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,845:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: S
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,847:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: VOLTAGE GET WEAKER AS IT DISCHARGES? MODIFY THE CIRCUIT SO THAT YOU CAN MEASURE
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,848:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: THE BATTERY
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,849:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,850:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: S VOLTAGE THE WHOLE TIME IT DISCHARGES.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,851:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: STARTING SETUP
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,863:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,875:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://pplines-online.bj.bcebos.com/deploy/official/paddleocr/pp-ocr-vl-16-online//89752177-4ead-46d8-b2ca-5f97ab47c47c/markdown_2/imgs/img_in_image_box_138_899_725_1516.jpg?authorization=bce-auth-v1%2FALTAKDN8mY5KlNI7zaRpLmOqrw%2F2026-08-11T07%3A56%3A32Z%2F-1%2F%2Ffed0e2b9e3df4160e2b47cb0dd58f5e8bc983131b67b07d7f13cd516fb9a0c8b" alt="Image" width="47%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,876:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: AVAILABLE PARTS
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,877:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,878:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 2 × Chain
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,879:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,880:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 1 × Capacitor (0.001 F)
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,881:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,882:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 1 × Junction
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,883:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: DIFFICULTY: ★★☆☆☆
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,884:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Tutorial 14: Voltage in Series Circuits
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,888:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: We already know that in a parallel circuit, the branches all feel the same voltage, but can have different current
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,890:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: running though them.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,891:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: In a series circuit, it
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,893:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,894:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: s the opposite
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,896:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: !
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,897:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,899:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: All the parts have the same current, but each part feels a different voltage.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,908:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,910:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: For instance, take a look at this circuit - theries a small resistor (200 Ω) and a large resistor (1000 Ω) in series.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,920:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,930:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://pplines-online.bj.bcebos.com/deploy/official/paddleocr/pp-ocr-vl-16-online//89752177-4ead-46d8-b2ca-5f97ab47c47c/markdown_3/imgs/img_in_image_box_236_610_737_964.jpg?authorization=bce-auth-v1%2FALTAKDN8mY5KlNI7zaRpLmOqrw%2F2026-08-11T07%3A56%3A33Z%2F-1%2F%2F7257a04468ad850ad5906a85328c6d5e5a52a31ad338eb5213e413158da69d44" alt="Image" width="40%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,933:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: The current through R1 is exactly the same as the current through R2. 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,934:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: "
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,936:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: How can that be?
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,937:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: "
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,939:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  you say. 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,940:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: "
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,942:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: R2 is a lot
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,943:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: harder to turn than R1
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,945:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: !
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,946:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: "
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,947:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  That
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,948:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,949:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: s true
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,949:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: !
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,950:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  It works because 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,952:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: R2 feels more of the battery
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,953:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,955:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: s voltage
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,959:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: . In this case, R2 feels
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,961:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: about 5 V while R1 feels only about 1 V.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,962:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: You can think about it another way. 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,964:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: R2 is larger, so it takes more voltage to get it turning the same speed as R1.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,965:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: CHALLENGE 45: VOLTAGE DIVIDER (2 V)
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,970:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: BUILD A CIRCUIT THAT SETS THE VOLTAGE ACROSS C1 TO APPROXIMATELY 2 V. (BETWEEN 2.0 V
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,972:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: AND 3.0 V IS ACCEPTABLE.)
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,974:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: THIS IS THE CIRCUIT YOU WILL BUILD:
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,982:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,994:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://pplines-online.bj.bcebos.com/deploy/official/paddleocr/pp-ocr-vl-16-online//8b6e3855-2548-4111-b368-31aa6d762a3b/markdown_0/imgs/img_in_image_box_341_217_525_481.jpg?authorization=bce-auth-v1%2FALTAKDN8mY5KlNI7zaRpLmOqrw%2F2026-08-11T07%3A56%3A32Z%2F-1%2F%2Fc8eacba099af291d301f123b0d47e85a3ce3d12d63f1beb58f56961506038034" alt="Image" width="20%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,995:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Ⓢ
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,995:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: I
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,996:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,997:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: ve got to remember to 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,998:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: tap
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:25,999:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,000:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: tap
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,000:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,001:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: tap
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,002:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  the voltmeter to get it to show an 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,004:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: accurate voltage
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,005:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: !
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,007:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: This is the symbol for a capacitor. Like this:
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,010:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,011:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://i.imgur.com/iGtOh46.png" width="10%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,012:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: STARTING SETUP
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,022:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,032:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://pplines-online.bj.bcebos.com/deploy/official/paddleocr/pp-ocr-vl-16-online//8b6e3855-2548-4111-b368-31aa6d762a3b/markdown_0/imgs/img_in_image_box_241_848_688_1549.jpg?authorization=bce-auth-v1%2FALTAKDN8mY5KlNI7zaRpLmOqrw%2F2026-08-11T07%3A56%3A32Z%2F-1%2F%2F4b94975e35feef9feab743ef85bfbf14d680eaadbde11ba1d95da94be72a4357" alt="Image" width="36%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,033:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: AVAILABLE PARTS
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,034:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,035:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 1 × 1000 Ω
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,037:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,038:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 4 × Chain
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,039:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,040:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 1 × 500 Ω
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,042:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,043:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 1 × Junction
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,044:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: DIFFICULTY: ★★☆☆☆
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,046:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: CHALLENGE 46: VOLTAGE DIVIDER (5 V)
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,049:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: BUILD A CIRCUIT THAT SETS THE VOLTAGE ACROSS C1 TO APPROXIMATELY 5 V. (BETWEEN 4.5 V
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,050:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: AND 5.5 V IS ACCEPTABLE.)
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,051:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: BONUS:
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,054:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  NOW BUILD A CIRCUIT THAT SETS THE VOLTAGE TO BETWEEN 1 AND 2 V.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,056:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Hmm...I get a more accurate voltage when I leave 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,058:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: just a little slack
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,060:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  in the chain.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,061:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: STARTING SETUP
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,070:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,080:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://pplines-online.bj.bcebos.com/deploy/official/paddleocr/pp-ocr-vl-16-online//8b6e3855-2548-4111-b368-31aa6d762a3b/markdown_1/imgs/img_in_image_box_274_845_751_1542.jpg?authorization=bce-auth-v1%2FALTAKDN8mY5KlNI7zaRpLmOqrw%2F2026-08-11T07%3A56%3A32Z%2F-1%2F%2F53ab166b72405f8bccebac13cf4215f9d038045087bab3688f0aeb7c1a2eafcb" alt="Image" width="38%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,082:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: AVAILABLE PARTS
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,083:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,084:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 1 × 1000 Ω
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,085:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,087:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 4 × Chain
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,088:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,090:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 1 × 200 Ω
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,091:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,093:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 1 × Junction
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,094:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: DIFFICULTY: ★★☆☆☆
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,095:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Tutorial 15: Kirchoff
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,096:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,097:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: s Law
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,097:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: This is one of those things that
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,098:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,101:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: s obvious once you understand it, but is hard figure it out on your own. Ready? Here
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,102:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: it is: 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,104:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: In any circuit loop, the sum of all the voltages is zero.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,106:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: What does that mean? Well, take a look at this circuit:
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,109:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,112:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://i.imgur.com/eziPNFB.png" alt="Image" width="38%"
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,113:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,118:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: The battery produces 6 V, and it pushes chain through a loop with three resistors. Each of those resistors push back
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,119:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: against the current. That 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,121:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: "
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,122:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: push back
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,124:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: "
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,125:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  is also a voltage - a 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,126:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: negative voltage
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,127:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: !
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,140:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  Bigger resistors generate more negative
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,142:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: voltage than smaller resistors. Add all the voltages in the loop together and what do you get? Zero spin volts
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,143:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: !
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,144:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: There
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,145:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,146:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: s a term for the negative voltage generated by each resistor. It
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,146:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,147:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: s called the 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,148:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: "
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,149:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: voltage drop
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,150:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: "
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,151:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: . That
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,152:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,167:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: s because the
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,176:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: voltage drops by that amount from one side of the resistor to the other. In the next picture, I drew in the voltage at
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,179:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: each point in the circuit.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,190:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,199:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://pplines-online.bj.bcebos.com/deploy/official/paddleocr/pp-ocr-vl-16-online//8b6e3855-2548-4111-b368-31aa6d762a3b/markdown_3/imgs/img_in_image_box_288_223_710_624.jpg?authorization=bce-auth-v1%2FALTAKDN8mY5KlNI7zaRpLmOqrw%2F2026-08-11T07%3A56%3A33Z%2F-1%2F%2F80dd569c8cc974793a56406fe630b4547b758d79720da79ca94b0ffb4cf87c90" alt="Image" width="34%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,201:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Ok, so now that you know about voltage drop, how do you measure it? You
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,203:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,204:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: ve got to measure the 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,206:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: difference in
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,207:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: voltage
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,209:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  from one side of the part to the other: the voltage 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,210:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: across
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,211:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  the part. Let
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,212:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,214:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: s measure the voltage drop across the
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,215:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 200 Ω resistor.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,216:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Can you do it like this, in series?
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,227:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,237:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://pplines-online.bj.bcebos.com/deploy/official/paddleocr/pp-ocr-vl-16-online//8b6e3855-2548-4111-b368-31aa6d762a3b/markdown_3/imgs/img_in_image_box_535_892_951_1241.jpg?authorization=bce-auth-v1%2FALTAKDN8mY5KlNI7zaRpLmOqrw%2F2026-08-11T07%3A56%3A33Z%2F-1%2F%2Faaf189c806967225b3bd29035cc8634949c6c581da95bd347a0a4b4d39913b1f" alt="Image" width="33%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,240:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Nope
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,241:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: !
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,242:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  The capacitor doesn
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,243:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,246:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: t let current through. Once it charges, it becomes the biggest resistance in the whole loop
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,247:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: and then you
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,248:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,249:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: re just measuring the voltage across it
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,250:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: !
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,251:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: To measure the voltage across a part, you
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,252:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,253:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: ve got to put the capacitor (voltmeter) 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,255:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: in parallel
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,257:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  with the part. Like this:
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,259:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,261:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://i.imgur.com/qvGXJvD.png"
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,262:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  width="60%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,264:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: At first, this was confusing for me. Doesn
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,265:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,266:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: t the capacitor create another path for current? It doesn
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,267:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,277:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: t really. The
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,280:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: capacitor charges up quickly and then blocks any more current from flowing through that path. It just charges to the 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,281:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: difference in voltage
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,282:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  from point A to point B. That is, it charges to 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,284:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: the voltage across the 200 Ω resistor
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,289:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: .
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,292:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: This still might be hard to understand, so try this. Build the circuit above, but take out the capacitor, like this:
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,300:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,311:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://pplines-online.bj.bcebos.com/deploy/official/paddleocr/pp-ocr-vl-16-online//3a9f314f-7449-4050-a25c-42b98851d62a/markdown_1/imgs/img_in_image_box_285_350_711_786.jpg?authorization=bce-auth-v1%2FALTAKDN8mY5KlNI7zaRpLmOqrw%2F2026-08-11T07%3A56%3A32Z%2F-1%2F%2F0ce4b289a587278d134f6103c7a8f9b29abac2d9f560ea03b8b78550533b7421" alt="Image" width="34%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,313:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Now, 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,314:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: hold the top sprocket
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,324:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  of the junction still with your fingers while you run the circuit. Remember, when one
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,326:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: sprocket of a junction is held still, the junction behaves like 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,328:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: nothing more than a series connection
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,329:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: !
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,332:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  All the current
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,334:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: going in one sprocket goes out the other sprocket.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,335:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: While you
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,337:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,339:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: re holding the top sprocket, can you feel how it
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,341:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,343:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: s trying to turn? What you
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,344:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,350:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: re feeling is the voltage
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,353:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: generated by the 200 Ω resistor pushing back against the current running through it. You
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,355:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,357:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: re actually 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,359:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: feeling the
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,360:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: voltage drop
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,360:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  across a resistor
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,361:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: !
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,362:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: CHALLENGE 47: VOLTAGE DROP
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,365:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: MODIFY THE CIRCUIT BELOW TO MEASURE THE VOLTAGE DROP ACROSS R2. IN OTHER WORDS,
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,366:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: MEASURE THE 
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,367:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: DIFFERENCE
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,368:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment:  IN VOLTAGE BETWEEN POINTS A AND B:
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,371:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,375:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://i.imgur.com/Hs5ZHek.png" width="40%"></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,377:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: Ⓢ
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,380:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: I need to measure the voltage across R2, so I
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,383:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: '
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,384:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: ll need to place a capacitor (voltmeter) in parallel with it.
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,386:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: STARTING SETUP
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,397:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,408:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://pplines-online.bj.bcebos.com/deploy/official/paddleocr/pp-ocr-vl-16-online//3a9f314f-7449-4050-a25c-42b98851d62a/markdown_2/imgs/img_in_image_box_225_827_750_1545.jpg?authorization=bce-auth-v1%2FALTAKDN8mY5KlNI7zaRpLmOqrw%2F2026-08-11T07%3A56%3A32Z%2F-1%2F%2F00c4c87928c9dab26736472e32982389cec066248986f76436fdbd7dfa29619d" alt="Image" width="42%" /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,410:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: AVAILABLE PARTS
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,411:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,412:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 3 × Chain
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,413:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,414:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 1 × Capacitor (0.001 F)
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,415:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: l
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,415:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: 1 × Junction
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,416:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: DIFFICULTY: ★★★☆☆
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,418:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: CHALLENGE 48: NOT GATE
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,426:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: WHEN SWITCH S1 IS ON, C1 SHOULD SHOW 0 V. WHEN S1 IS OFF, C1 SHOULD SHOW
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,428:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: APPROXIMATELY 6 V. (NEGATIVE 6 V) IS ALSO ACCEPTABLE.)
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,431:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: <div style="text-align: center;"><img
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,432:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: src="https://i.imgur.com/8HYL2my.png" alt="Image" width="50%"
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,434:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: /></div>
   DEBUG: 2026-08-12 00:22:26,435:      pdf_renderer.py:  470: printSegment: The output is the opposite of the input? This could be useful...
